A work that reproduces, closely follows, or is substantially derived from the Corresponding Source, architecture, evidence-handling pipeline, or Protected Safety Mechanism integration points of Xabat is a Modified Version subject to this License, regardless of renaming, refactoring, translation into another programming language, or other cosmetic changes made with the purpose or effect of avoiding the obligations of this License.

## 3. Protected Safety Mechanisms

**3.1. Safety Preservation**

The safety architecture of Xabat is a fundamental condition of the license.

You may improve, replace, repair, or modify a Protected Safety Mechanism provided that the resulting mechanism provides equivalent or stronger protection.

**3.2. Prohibition on Material Weakening**

You may not Distribute or Host a Modified Version in which a Protected Safety Mechanism has been subjected to a Material Safety Modification.

**3.3. Equivalent Replacements**

Replacing a Protected Safety Mechanism with another system is permitted when the replacement provides equivalent or stronger protection.

A disabled function, empty implementation, ineffective placeholder, or intentionally weaker substitute does not satisfy this requirement.

**3.4. Good-Faith Presumption for Replacements**

A replacement of a Protected Safety Mechanism is presumed to satisfy Section 3.3 if it is selected and implemented in Good Faith, uses an actively maintained detection or safeguard system reasonably appropriate to its purpose, and is not demonstrably and substantially less effective than the mechanism it replaces. This presumption may be rebutted by clear evidence that the replacement was selected for the purpose, or with the effect, of materially weakening protection. A disabled function, empty implementation, or placeholder with no real detection or protective capability is never covered by this presumption.

**3.5. Purpose**

These requirements exist to prevent Xabat from being converted into software intended to facilitate grooming, sexual exploitation, CSAM-related abuse, uncontrolled evidence collection, or uncontrolled retention of Sensitive Evidence.

## 4. Prohibited Uses

You may not use, modify, distribute, or host Xabat for the purpose of:

a. facilitating grooming or sexual exploitation of minors;

b. facilitating child sexual abuse;

c. knowingly facilitating the unlawful acquisition, distribution, storage, or access of Child Sexual Abuse Material (CSAM);

d. creating or operating an uncontrolled repository intended to collect or retain exploitative material;

e. intentionally circumventing Protected Safety Mechanisms;

f. intentionally using the software to target minors for sexual exploitation;

g. intentionally configuring or modifying the Program to defeat its evidence-retention, deletion, access-control, or abuse-prevention safeguards for an abusive purpose; or

h. knowingly assisting, instructing, or facilitating another person's Prohibited Use under this Section.

Nothing in this section prohibits good-faith handling of Sensitive Evidence as reasonably necessary for legitimate reporting, moderation, investigation, safety, or legal compliance, including voluntary or legally required disclosure to law enforcement, the National Center for Missing & Exploited Children, or an equivalent reporting body.

No separate agreement, terms of service, contract, or waiver may override, narrow, or excuse compliance with this Section or with Sections 3 or 5. Any such attempted waiver is void and without effect under this License.

## 5. Evidence Handling and Retention

**5.1. Retention Safeguards**

When Hosting Xabat, you must maintain the designated evidence-retention and deletion safeguards provided by the project.

**5.2. Uncontrolled Retention**

You may not intentionally configure or modify Xabat to create permanent, uncontrolled archives of Sensitive Evidence.

**5.3. Safety Improvements**

Operators may implement stronger deletion, retention, access-control, encryption, or evidence-isolation mechanisms than those included in the original Program.

**5.4. Necessary Handling**

Nothing in this section requires the immediate destruction of evidence when temporary retention is reasonably necessary for legitimate moderation, safety, reporting, investigation, or compliance with applicable law.

**5.5. No Model Training on Sensitive Evidence**

You may not use Sensitive Evidence, or a dataset derived from it, to train, fine-tune, evaluate, or otherwise develop a machine learning model, classifier, or similar automated system, except for the Program's own Protected Safety Mechanisms operating for their designated detection purpose within the Program itself.

**5.6. Mandatory Reporting Compliance**

If you Host the Program or a Modified Version, you must comply with all Applicable Reporting Law in every jurisdiction where you operate, and you may not configure or modify the Program in a manner that prevents, delays, or interferes with your ability to meet those obligations.

**5.7. Access Controls for Hosted Instances**

If you Host the Program or a Modified Version, you must restrict access to Sensitive Evidence to personnel with a legitimate operational need and must maintain a reasonable access log sufficient to identify who accessed Sensitive Evidence and when.

## 6. Source Availability for Hosted Modified Versions

**6.1. Private Use**

Running Xabat privately for personal use, testing, development, or internal purposes does not by itself create an obligation to provide Corresponding Source to third parties.

**6.2. Public Hosting**

If you Host a Modified Version as a publicly accessible network service that allows third parties to interact with it remotely, you must provide those users with a reasonable and customary means of obtaining the Corresponding Source of the Modified Version actually being Hosted.

**6.3. Availability**

Corresponding Source must be provided without charge, other than reasonable physical distribution costs where physical distribution is requested.

The source must remain available for as long as the applicable Modified Version remains publicly Hosted.

**6.4. Source Location**

A publicly Hosted Modified Version should provide a clear mechanism, such as a source link or equivalent notice, through which users can locate the Corresponding Source.

## 7. Termination and Reinstatement

**7.1. Termination**

Your rights under this License terminate automatically upon material violation of its terms.

**7.2. Curable Violations**

For violations that are reasonably capable of being cured, your license may be reinstated if you cease the violating activity and fully cure the violation within 30 days after becoming aware of the violation.

**7.3. Intentional Safety Circumvention**

Intentional circumvention of Protected Safety Mechanisms constitutes a material violation.

A person who intentionally distributes or Hosts a Modified Version after removing or materially weakening Protected Safety Mechanisms may not rely upon the cure provision merely by ceasing distribution after the violation has occurred.

**7.4. Prohibited Use**

Use of Xabat for a Prohibited Use constitutes a material violation of this License.

A Prohibited Use under Section 4 is not eligible for cure under Section 7.2, for the same reasons set out in Section 7.3.

**7.5. Effect of Termination**

Upon termination, you no longer have permission under this License to use, modify, Distribute, or Host the Program.

Termination does not retroactively terminate rights that were validly granted to third parties before the violation, except to the extent otherwise permitted by applicable law.

